Mill Extras

Optional files for Mill that need nothing from it, and that it needs nothing from: Mill runs
the same without them.

Web Fonts
  A font suitcase (type FFIL, creator MiLL) of two bitmap font families, Mill Serif and Mill
  Mono. Put it in the "Mill Plug-Ins" folder beside Mill. At startup Mill opens each FFIL file
  there, read-only, and keeps it open; the font settings (General, Proportional font and Fixed
  font) then list the families in every such file with the System's, and Mill Serif and Mill Mono are
  the defaults (without the suitcase, Times and Courier). It is skipped where the System has a 36 point Times
  already (System 7 with TrueType), and when Shift is held at startup, as Plug-Ins are. What is
  in it is checked first (every table of each FONT and FOND inside the resource, every FONT the
  FOND names); a family that fails, or a file that can't be opened, is left out, and Mill uses
  the System's fonts as if it weren't there.

  Two families, each in regular, bold, and italic (bold italic is made by QuickDraw; Mill Mono's
  bold and italic stop at 14 point), at 9, 10,
  12, 14, 18, 24, and 36 point, the sizes HTML 3.2 asks for:
    Mill Serif   Adobe's X11 bitmaps of the Times design: timR, timB, timI
    Mill Mono    Adobe's X11 bitmaps of the Courier design: courR, courB, courO (the oblique
                 is the italic)
  Each size slot takes its strike from the "files" entry in fonts.json (a source and a BDF file
  for each slot). Now: slot 9 is the 75 dpi 10 pixel strike, 10 is the 100 dpi 8 point (11
  pixels), 12, 14, 18, and 24 are the 75 dpi 12, 14, 18, and 24 pixel strikes, and 36 is the 100
  dpi 24 point (34 pixels); the table under Styles gives each slot's pixel size. The strikes are
  Adobe's hand-tuned bitmaps, converted, not redrawn: each Mac Roman character 32 to 255 is
  found by its Unicode value in the BDF file (its encoding is ISO 10646-1) and put in its Mac
  Roman place. The characters a strike lacks show as the missing glyph: the fi and fl ligatures
  and the Apple logo in every strike, and, in most strikes, ten math symbols (infinity, partial, sum, product, pi, integral,
  Omega, root, almost equal, increment) and the lozenge; build.py prints the list.
  Mill measures and draws a line in them with the line height the System's Times or Courier
  would give, so pages don't shift. Mill uses them only where a setting names them; a page's
  FACE names only the System's fonts.

  It can also be opened with Font/DA Mover; the Bold and Italic strikes are FONTs of their own
  (see Styles).

Making it
  python3 build.py [--config fonts.json] [--out dist] [--cache cache] [--preview] [--styles] [--table]
  Needs Python 3 with freetype-py and macresources (and Pillow for --preview), for example in
  the python:3.12-slim image: pip install freetype-py macresources Pillow.
  It downloads the font releases named in fonts.json (x.org's font-adobe-75dpi and
  font-adobe-100dpi 1.0.4), checks each SHA-256, converts the BDF strikes, and writes:
    Web Fonts.bin           MacBinary, with the fonts, 'vers' 1 and 2, and 'TEXT' 128 (about)
                             and 'TXGZ' 129 (the license, gzipped)
    Web Fonts Read Me.txt   the same, with the license, for the folder it goes in
  Nothing from the fonts' releases is kept in this repository. A family with "ttf" instead of
  "files" in fonts.json is rendered with FreeType (a "hinting" entry then picks, per size, the
  font's own hints or the autohinter); the default configuration has none.

Styles
  Mill Serif has real Bold and Italic strikes at every size; Mill Mono at 9, 10, 12, and 14 point
  (QuickDraw makes them from the plain strike above that). They are FONTs of their own, found
  through the FOND's association table at an ID in an unused family; the table lists the slot
  sizes with the strikes inside them. Underline is QuickDraw's.
  build.py refuses a style whose real strikes are not the family's smallest sizes with no gap: on a
  Mac Plus, real Bold at only 18, 24, and 36 point hung the machine, and the cause is not known
  (all sizes, and 9, 10, and 12 of 9 to 36, are tested and fine). --table prints what each strike costs:

  FONT resource bytes          9      10      12      14      18      24      36   total
  Mill Serif plain          2568    2594    3240    3854    5946    9514   16100   43816
  Mill Serif bold           2620    2646    3300    4098    5886    9218   15656   43424
  Mill Serif italic         2698    2698    3300    4170    5946    9920   16026   44758
  Mill Mono plain           2228    2228    2862    3618    4806    7362   12066   35170
  Mill Mono bold            2426    2426    3114    3874       -       -       -   11840
  Mill Mono italic          2404    2360    3142    3778       -       -       -   11684
  Mill Serif pixels           10      11      12      14      18      24      34
  Mill Mono pixels            10      11      12      14      18      24      34

  suitcase, plain only: 82847 bytes; with every real style listed: 195493 bytes

More fonts: add an entry to "fonts" in fonts.json: family (a name of your own), id (the font
family number, 128 to 255, one that isn't a family on the Systems you target; FONT resources
are id * 128 + size, so each style's strike needs an unused one too; the script checks they are
all different), source (one in "sources"), ttf (a file in it), from (the name to credit), sizes.

